## Correct Answer:
<p>**Option:** Invoke async operation with Reactor context: client.listTools().contextWrite(ctx -> ctx.put(McpTransportContext.KEY, McpTransportContext.create(Map.of("some-key","some value"))).put("reactor-context-key","some reactor value"));<br> ensure ExchangeFilterFunction and McpAsyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer use Mono.deferContextual.</p>
<p>You must insert the McpTransportContext into the Reactor context for each async client operation using contextWrite so request-scoped data is available to filters and customizers; doing client.listTools().contextWrite(... put(McpTransportContext.KEY, ...)) makes the McpTransportContext retrievable by McpAsyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer or an ExchangeFilterFunction via Mono.deferContextual, allowing them to compute per-request headers and metadata rather than producing missing or incorrect request values.</p>
## Incorrect Options:
### Distractor 1:
<p>Set McpTransportContext in a ThreadLocal before calling the async client and have your filters read it at request time: TRANSPORT.set(McpTransportContext.create(Map.of("some-key","some value")));<br> client.listTools(); TRANSPORT.remove();

This tries to reuse a synchronous ThreadLocal for request-scoped data.</p>
<p>In Reactor-based async flows the execution may run on different threads, so ThreadLocal values typically won't be visible inside ExchangeFilterFunction or McpAsyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer; you must use Reactor Context propagation (contextWrite / Mono.deferContextual) instead.</p>
### Distractor 2:
<p>Set the McpTransportContext as a Reactor Netty / connection attribute on the underlying HTTP client and have filters read the Netty Channel attribute at request time. Example: ((ReactorNettyTransport) transport).httpClient().doOnRequest((req, conn) -> conn.channel().attr(McpTransportContext.KEY).set(McpTransportContext.create(Map.of("some-key","some value"))));<br> client.listTools();

This puts per-request data on the Netty Channel (connection) instead of in the Reactor Context.</p>
<p>Channel attributes are tied to a TCP connection and can be shared or reused across multiple requests (connection pooling), so they can leak between requests or be missing when connections are recycled.</p>
<p>Also ExchangeFilterFunction / McpAsyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer operate at the reactive client layer and do not reliably read transport-level channel attributes; the correct concept is to propagate request-scoped data in the Reactor Context (contextWrite / Mono.deferContextual).</p>
### Distractor 3:
<p>Compute and inject auth tokens synchronously inside an ExchangeFilterFunction (blocking call) and set headers immediately.<br>Example: ExchangeFilterFunction authFilter = (request, next) -> {
    // blocking synchronous token fetch (e.g., legacy auth client)
    String token = LegacyAuthClient.getToken(request.headers().asHttpHeaders().getFirst("x-some-in"));
    ClientRequest mutated = ClientRequest.from(request).header("Authorization", "Bearer " + token).build();
    return next.exchange(mutated);
}; WebClient.builder().filter(authFilter).build(); client.listTools();

This uses a blocking, synchronous token fetch inside a reactive filter.</p>
<p>While it may appear to inject headers per-request, blocking inside Reactor/Netty event-loop threads causes thread starvation, latency spikes, and possible deadlocks under high throughput. More importantly for this question, a synchronous fetch in the filter bypasses Reactor Context propagation (it doesn't retrieve McpTransportContext via Mono.deferContextual), so it won't reliably access per-request data placed in the Reactor Context — headers can still be missing or incorrect.</p>
<p>The correct approach is to retrieve McpTransportContext non-blockingly from the Reactor Context (Mono.deferContextual / contextWrite) and compose any token fetch as a non-blocking Mono.</p>
